Keep a seeder<->seeder connection for a 5 s grace period after both sides
are complete, instead of dropping it at once.

Everything above the first "diff --git" line is commentary; git apply skips
it, so this file still applies exactly like the six before it.

WHY (the peer-transfer capture race)

  IRIS attributes staged bytes per peer by reading aria2's own session
  counters (getPeers "downloaded"/"uploaded") from the
  --on-bt-download-complete hook, device/agent/peer-transfer-hook.sh. The
  hook is exec'd from src/DefaultPieceStorage.cc:501-506 the instant the
  last piece lands, and its aria2.getPeers call reaches aria2's single
  threaded RPC loop only after a fork+exec+curl round trip.

  Upstream drops every seeder peer before that call can be served.
  src/DefaultBtInteractive.cc:510-513 ("If both of us are seeders, drop
  connection." -> throw DL_ABORT_EX(MSG_GOOD_BYE_SEEDER)) fires on the very
  next event-loop iteration after completion, and
  src/DefaultPeerStorage.cc:339-341 (returnPeer: usedPeers_.erase /
  onErasingPeer) removes the peer from the table getPeers enumerates
  (src/RpcMethodImpl.cc:1078). On a single-vCPU Catalyst 8000V IOx app the
  hook's round trip measured 21-50 ms idle, and every download there
  produced an empty peer list and zero attributed bytes, while faster
  devices sometimes won the race (board #68). A device fed only by seeders
  -- the first device of every wave, fed by the origin -- is the worst case.

  The far end drops too, and sooner. checkHave() advertises every completed
  piece to every peer (src/DefaultPieceStorage.cc:724-742 has no per-CUID
  filter), so the finishing side's HAVE or BITFIELD for its last piece goes
  out in the same iteration, and the peer that receives it throws the same
  MSG_GOOD_BYE_SEEDER at src/BtHaveMessage.cc:62, src/BtBitfieldMessage.cc:85
  or src/BtHaveAllMessage.cc:68 the moment its view of us becomes complete.
  Measured on loopback with both ends running a build that patched only
  DefaultBtInteractive: the seeder was still listed at the completion
  instant and gone 10 ms later, closed from its end, and the hook still
  captured an empty list. The origin runs this same binary, so the grace
  has to hold on both ends of the connection.

WHAT

  DefaultBtInteractive gains seedersSinceTimer_: zero until the first
  checkActiveInteraction() iteration that observes both sides complete, and
  the Good Bye Seeder drop fires only once that state has held for
  SEEDER_GOODBYE_GRACE (5 s). The timer returns to zero if the condition
  stops holding. The existing 30 s mutual-disinterest and 60 s inactivity
  drops just above it are untouched and still apply during the grace.

  The three message handlers no longer throw on the message that completes
  the peer's copy; they leave the drop to checkActiveInteraction(), which
  runs on every interaction iteration for the same connection, so the same
  condition now ends the connection after the same grace from either side.

  Peers that never announce a complete copy (the loopback peer-cap fixtures
  send only a handshake) are unaffected. The visible cost is that a finished
  torrent holds each seeder slot for up to 5 s longer before freeing it,
  and a seeder that connects to an already finished torrent is turned away
  after 5 s instead of at its BITFIELD.

